# Every arithmetic candidate, and whether it lands in exactly one verdict class. One row per site.
#
# Checked by key, not by count
#   A count-only partition check is insufficient in principle, and this one is by key.
#   Σ|classes| = |candidates| passes when a tuple in two classes is cancelled by a tuple in none.
#   The classes must be pairwise disjoint and jointly exhaustive, which is a statement about each
#   candidate, not about the totals. So: group by (site, filing, layer) and require exactly one.
#
# Not exhaustive is the silent failure
#   A candidate falling through every CASE branch takes a NULL verdict, and the site then reports
#   a smaller population and a cleaner verdict than it earned: the same failure
#   `violates IS NULL` is guarded against in checks/.
#
# The NULL filter sits inside the count
#   Never beside it. As a `WHERE` it removes the whole group, and a candidate in no class stops
#   being a candidate; as a `FILTER` it leaves the group with zero classes and `classes <> 1`
#   catches it. The two spellings are one keyword apart and they make opposite claims about
#   exhaustiveness.
#
# Which classes exist is the type's job
#   No count can do it. "Exactly one class" survives any renaming of the classes, a misspelt one
#   included, and a tally that selects a class by name then drops it. The classes are
#   `public.arithmetic_verdict`, and every arm casts to it, so a misspelling fails when the query
#   is parsed, on every run, whether or not any candidate reaches that arm.
#
# Probe
#   examples/probes runs it as `arithmetic_class`. Drop the `ELSE` that makes a remainder
#   `computable` in arithmetic/remainder.sqlc: its candidates fall out of every class, and the law
#   must fail for `r = n - d` alone. With the filter beside the count it would still return `t`.
-- arithmetic/all.sqlc, one candidate to exactly one verdict.
SELECT a.slug AS law, p.subject, p.holds, p.detail
FROM      (
    :compose(algebra/roster.sqlc)
) a
LEFT JOIN (
    SELECT z.site AS subject,
           count(*) FILTER (WHERE z.classes <> 1) = 0 AS holds,
           format('%s candidates, %s in exactly one class', count(*),
                  count(*) FILTER (WHERE z.classes = 1)) AS detail
    FROM ( SELECT site, filing, layer,
                  count(*) FILTER (WHERE w.verdict IS NOT NULL) AS classes
           FROM ( :compose(arithmetic/all.sqlc) ) w
           GROUP BY site, filing, layer ) z
    GROUP BY z.site
) p ON true
WHERE a.slug = 'arithmetic_class'
